      content: `A splash cymbal will never be a defining voice in a sludge metal setup the way it is in progressive or technical metal — the genre's whole identity is built around the crash-and-ride tonnage that drummers like Brann Dailor, Igor Cavalera, and Mario Duplantier anchor their sound around instead. But used sparingly, a dark-voiced splash can genuinely sharpen a single transition or accent without working against sludge's crushing sense of weight.

If you decide a splash belongs in your setup, start with a dark, 10"-12" voicing that matches your existing crash character, and resist the urge to reach for it often. In sludge metal, restraint is the real skill — the cymbal choice is secondary to knowing when not to play it.

🤘 **Stay heavy, stay patient, and let the weight crush through.**`
