      content: `"Extreme metal" spans death, thrash, and black metal — genres with very different riffing styles that all converge on the same non-negotiable pedal requirement: sustained, blast-beat-capable speed that doesn't fall apart over a full set. Whether it's George Kollias sustaining 240+ BPM single-stroke blasts with Nile, Gene Hoglan proving decades of metronomic consistency across Death and Testament, Derek Roddy earning the nickname "One Take" recording extreme-speed material in single passes, or Dave Lombardo laying down the foundational thrash-into-extreme-metal blueprint on "Reign in Blood," the baseline demand is identical: a pedal that responds instantly and identically, blast after blast, riff after riff.

That baseline is what separates a genuine extreme metal pedal from a general-purpose metal pedal. Lower-tempo genres can get away with heavier springs and softer beaters; extreme metal cannot. You need low-friction bearings, a drive system that doesn't fatigue your ankle at sustained high tempo, and a beater surface that cuts cleanly through a wall of downtuned guitars at 200+ BPM.

This guide covers exactly what makes a bass drum pedal work across extreme metal's blast-beat baseline — genre-agnostic requirements grounded in what its fastest, most reliable drummers actually play — with recommendations across every budget.`,
