        answer: "Heel-toe technique lets a drummer's foot rock between two contact points on the footboard — heel down, then toe — effectively doubling the number of strokes available per motion cycle compared to ankle-only playing. It requires an extended longboard footboard because a standard-length board doesn't give the heel and toe enough separate room to rock between reliably at extreme tempo."
